# Kinematics — Motion Graphs

Lesson 4 of 4

---

## Learning Objective

Students will be able to read position-time and velocity-time graphs, find velocity and acceleration from slopes, and find displacement from the area under a velocity-time graph.

---

## Review: Acceleration

- **Acceleration** is the rate at which velocity changes: $a = \dfrac{v - v_0}{t}$.
- Units: $\frac{\mathrm{m}}{\mathrm{s}^2}$.
- Same direction as motion → speeding up; opposite → slowing down.
- With constant acceleration: $v = v_0 + a t$.

---
class: g7
---

## Motion Graphs

The motion of an object can be shown on a graph with **time** on the $x$-axis:

- **Position-time graph** — how position changes with time
- **Velocity-time graph** — how velocity changes with time

---
class: g7
---

## Position vs Time

<img src="/x-t_graphs.excalidraw.svg" width="720" />

- The $y$-value tells you the **position** at a given time.
- Read across from the time, then up to the line.

---
class: g7
---

## Velocity from a Position-Time Graph

On a **position-time** graph, the **slope** is the velocity.

$$v = \frac{\Delta x}{t} = \frac{x - x_0}{t}$$

- A steeper slope means a **greater velocity**.
> The subscript "$_0$" means _initial_ while no subscript means final.
> Initial position $=x_0$ and final position $=x$.

---
class: g7
---

## Comparing Steepness: Velocity

<img src="/x-t_graphs.excalidraw.svg" width="720" />

<v-clicks>

- The **steeper** the line, the **greater** the velocity.
- A **flat** line means the object is **at rest** ($v = 0$).

</v-clicks>

---
class: g7
---

## Velocity vs Time

<img src="/v-t_graphs.excalidraw.svg" width="720" />

- The $y$-value tells you the **velocity** at a given time.
- Read across from the time, then up to the line.

---
class: g7
---

## Constant vs Changing Motion

On a **velocity-time** graph:

<v-clicks>

- A **flat (horizontal) line** → velocity is **constant** (not changing).
- A **sloped line** → velocity is **changing** (the object is accelerating).

</v-clicks>

---
class: g7
---

## Acceleration from a Velocity-Time Graph

On a **velocity-time** graph, the **slope** is the acceleration.

$$a = \frac{\Delta v}{t} = \frac{v - v_0}{t}$$
> Initial velocity $=v_0$ and final velocity $=v$.

---
class: g7
---

## Comparing Steepness: Acceleration

<img src="/v-t_graphs.excalidraw.svg" width="720" />

<v-clicks>

- The **steeper** the line, the **greater** the acceleration.
- A **flat** line means **zero acceleration** (constant velocity).

</v-clicks>

---
class: g7
---

## Example

Use the velocity-time graph to answer:

<v-clicks>

- Which part of the graph shows **constant velocity**?
- Which part shows the object **speeding up**?

</v-clicks>

<img src="/v-t_graphs.excalidraw.svg" width="600" />

---
class: g7
---

## Area Under the Curve

The **area** between the line on a velocity-time graph and the horizontal axis equals the **displacement**.

<v-clicks>

- Rectangle: area $= \text{base} \times \text{height} = t \times v$
- Triangle: area $= \tfrac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}$

</v-clicks>

---
class: g7
---

## Units of Area Under the Curve

$$ \text{area} = \text{velocity} \times \text{time} = \frac{\mathrm{m}}{\mathrm{s}} \times \mathrm{s} = \mathrm{m} $$

- The units are **meters** — the units of **displacement**.

---
class: g7
---

## Example: Area and Displacement

A cart moves at a constant $v = 4 \frac{\mathrm{m}}{\mathrm{s}}$ for $3 \mathrm{s}$.

$$ \text{area} = 4 \frac{\mathrm{m}}{\mathrm{s}} \times 3 \mathrm{s} = 12 \mathrm{m} $$

The displacement is $12 \mathrm{m}$.

---

## Summary

- **Position-time** graphs show position; **velocity-time** graphs show velocity.
- On a **position-time** graph, **slope = velocity**.
- On a **velocity-time** graph, **slope = acceleration**.
- A flat line on a velocity-time graph means **constant velocity**.
- **Area** under a velocity-time graph = **displacement**; its units are meters.
